Task 5.3 – Compare the Four Styles (2 Marks)
1. Comparison Table
Parameter	Frame implements ActionListener	Named inner class	Anonymous inner class	Lambda expression
Readability	Moderate	Good	Moderate	Very good
Code length	More code	More code	Longer	Short
Access to frame fields	Direct	Direct	Direct	Direct
Handler reuse	Possible	Easy	Possible	Easy
Ease of modification	Moderate	High	Moderate	High
Suitable for large applications	Less suitable	Very suitable	Less suitable	Very suitable

2. Explanation
(a) Frame implements ActionListener
- The main JFrame itself handles events.
- It is simple for small applications.
- When many buttons are added, the actionPerformed() method can become lengthy.
(b) Named Inner Class
- The handler has a separate class name.
- It can be reused for multiple components.
- It is easier to maintain when event-handling logic becomes complex.
(c) Anonymous Inner Class
- It avoids creating a separate named class.
- The event-handling logic is written near the component registration.
- Large event-handling code can make the constructor difficult to read.
(d) Lambda Expression
- Provides concise event-handling code.
- Reduces unnecessary boilerplate.
- Suitable for simple event-handling operations.
- Makes the program easier to read when the handler performs a small task.
3. Which Style Is Best for a Large Application?
I would prefer the named inner class or a separate reusable Action class for a large application.

The reason is that large applications contain multiple buttons, menu items and keyboard shortcuts. Named handlers allow event-handling logic to be organized separately and reused across different components. They also make debugging and future modifications easier.

Lambda expressions are preferable for short and simple event-handling operations.

Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
1. Alternative Approach: Method Reference
Instead of writing a lambda expression:
ActionListener handler = e -> convert();


We can use a method reference:
ActionListener handler = e -> this.convert();


For a method reference, the method must have a compatible signature with ActionListener.actionPerformed(ActionEvent).

Since convert() takes no arguments, directly writing this::convert is not compatible with ActionListener.

A correct method-reference solution is:
private void handleConvert(ActionEvent e) {
    convert();
}


Register it using:
ActionListener handler = this::handleConvert;

convertButton.addActionListener(handler);
amount.addActionListener(handler);


This approach reduces the need for a separate lambda body and makes the handler method reusable.
2. Alternative Approach: Shared Swing Action Object
Swing provides the AbstractAction class, which allows the same action to be shared by multiple components.

Example:

import javax.swing.*;
import java.awt.*;
import java.awt.event.*;

public class ConverterAction extends JFrame {

    JTextField amount = new JTextField(10);

    JComboBox<String> target =
        new JComboBox<>(new String[]{"USD", "EUR"});

    JLabel result = new JLabel(" ");

    public ConverterAction() {

        setTitle("Currency Converter");
        setLayout(new FlowLayout());

        Action convertAction = new AbstractAction("Convert") {

            @Override
            public void actionPerformed(ActionEvent e) {
                convert();
            }
        };

        JButton convertButton = new JButton(convertAction);

        amount.addActionListener(convertAction);

        add(new JLabel("INR"));
        add(amount);
        add(target);
        add(convertButton);
        add(result);

        JMenuBar menuBar = new JMenuBar();
        JMenu menu = new JMenu("Currency");

        JMenuItem menuItem = new JMenuItem(convertAction);

        menu.add(menuItem);
        menuBar.add(menu);

        setJMenuBar(menuBar);

        setDefaultCloseOperation(EXIT_ON_CLOSE);
        pack();
    }

    private void convert() {

        try {

            double value = Double.parseDouble(amount.getText());

            if (!Double.isFinite(value) || value < 0) {
                throw new NumberFormatException();
            }

            String currency = (String) target.getSelectedItem();

            double rate = currency.equals("USD") ? 0.012 : 0.011;

            String message = String.format(
                "%.2f %s", value * rate, currency
            );

            result.setText(message);

            JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(this, message);

        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {

            result.setText("Invalid amount");

            JOptionPane.showMessageDialog(
                this, "Invalid amount",
                "Input Error", JOptionPane.ERROR_MESSAGE
            );
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        SwingUtilities.invokeLater(() ->
            new ConverterAction().setVisible(true));
    }
}



Explanation:
- AbstractAction creates a reusable action object.
- The Convert button uses the action.
- The amount field uses the same action when Enter is pressed.
- A menu item also uses the same action.
- The same operation can be connected to keyboard shortcuts through Swing's key bindings.
3. Time and Space Complexity Analysis
Let k represent the number of components or triggers that invoke the Convert action.
Time Complexity
Approach	Event handling time	Explanation
Frame implements ActionListener	O(1)	Directly invokes the frame's handler.
Named inner class	O(1)	Directly invokes the named handler.
Anonymous inner class	O(1)	Directly invokes the anonymous handler.
Lambda expression	O(1)	Directly invokes the lambda handler.
Method reference	O(1)	Directly invokes the referenced handler method.
Shared Action object	O(1)	Each registered component invokes the same action.

All these approaches have constant-time event dispatch overhead, excluding the actual conversion calculation and dialog display.
Space Complexity
Approach	Additional handler space	Explanation
Frame implements ActionListener	O(1)	The frame itself acts as the listener.
Named inner class	O(1)	One handler object can be shared.
Anonymous inner class	O(1)	One anonymous listener object can be shared.
Lambda expression	O(1)	One shared listener reference is used.
Method reference	O(1)	One shared method-reference listener is used.
Shared Action object	O(1)	One Action object is shared among components.

If k separate listener objects are created instead of sharing one, the additional listener storage becomes O(k).
